                ----- SUMMARY -----                               9228 1451   21
                                                                  9228 1451   22
Major features of the ENDF/B-VII evaluation are:                  9228 1451   23
1. A new evaluation of the (n,f) cross section based on ENDF/B-   9228 1451   24
   VII standard cross section analysis is incorporated.           9228 1451   25
2. A new evaluation above thermal energy of prompt nubar          9228 1451   26
   consistent with experimental data within uncertainties and     9228 1451   27
   with fast critical benchmark measurements is included.         9228 1451   28
3. New unresolved resonance parameter data are incorporated.      9228 1451   29
4. A new analysis of the prompt fission neutron spectrum matrix   9228 1451   30
   based on the Los Alamos model is used to calculate neutron     9228 1451   31
   spectra at all energies. [The ENDF/B-VI results from the Los   9228 1451   32
   Alamos model are used for the thermal spectrum.]               9228 1451   33
5. Improved delayed neutron data are incorporated.                9228 1451   34
6. New reaction theory calculations are utilized for (n,xn) and   9228 1451   35
   other reactions.  Direct reaction cross sections and angular   9228 1451   36
   distributions are extended to an excitation energy of 4 MeV.   9228 1451   37
7. Improved fission energy release values are incorporated.       9228 1451   38
                                                                  9228 1451   39
             ----- DETAILED DESCRIPTION -----                     9228 1451   40
                                                                  9228 1451   41
>> MF=1 GENERAL INFORMATION                                       9228 1451   42
                                                                  9228 1451   43
MT=452: AVERAGE TOTAL NUMBER OF NEUTRONS PER FISSION (TOTAL       9228 1451   44
        NUBAR)                                                    9228 1451   45
   Sum of MT=455 and 456. The ENDF/B-VI values are preserved      9228 1451   46
below 1 eV.                                                       9228 1451   47
                                                                  9228 1451   48
MT=455: DELAYED NEUTRON DATA                                      9228 1451   49
   The decay constants and abundances are based on new delayed    9228 1451   50
neutron 6-group parameters from CINDER'90 summation calculations. 9228 1451   51
The CINDER calculations (Wi05) utilize a new CINDER library in    9228 1451   52
which beta-decay half-lives and beta-delayed neutron-emission     9228 1451   53
probablities are obtained from the evaluated experimental data    9228 1451   54
file NuBase2003 (Au03), when available there. When experimental   9228 1451   55
data are not available, the data are calculated in a model where  9228 1451   56
allowed Gamow-Teller decays are treated in a microscopic quasi-   9228 1451   57
particle random-phase approximation (QRPA) and the first          9228 1451   58
forbidden decays are treated in the statistical gross theory      9228 1451   59
(Mo03).                                                           9228 1451   60
   The delayed nubar values were carried over from ENDF/B-VI,     9228 1451   61
except between 1.0-5 eV and 50 keV where they were modified to    9228 1451   62
agree with JENDL-3.3 values, following a suggestion from C.       9228 1451   63
Lubitz (Lu06).                                                    9228 1451   64
                                                                  9228 1451   65
MT=456: AVERAGE NUMER OF PROMPT NEUTRONS RELEASED PER FISSION     9228 1451   66
   1.e-5 eV - 10 keV:  Very similar to ENDF/B-VI. Minor changes   9228 1451   67
from the ENDF/B-VI values were made to more closely approximate   9228 1451   68
the energy dependence of the JENDL-3.3 evaluation and to          9228 1451   69
accommodate our change in delayed nubar (above) while at the same 9228 1451   70
time keeping total nubar unchanged.                               9228 1451   71
   10 keV - 20 MeV:  Generally based on results from the          9228 1451   72
covariance analysis of experimental data performed for ENDF/B-VI, 9228 1451   73
after renormalization of the data above 6-8 MeV for consistency   9228 1451   74
with the ENDF/B-VII standard 252Cf nubar value.                   9228 1451   75
(Between 10 and 50 keV, small changes were also included to       9228 1451   76
compensate for the delayed nubar change described above.) We      9228 1451   77
attempted to follow the covariance data as well as possible but   9228 1451   78
mainly to stay within uncertainties in the data and to keep good  9228 1451   79
agreement with fast critical benchmarks. The structure in the     9228 1451   80
Version VI covariance analysis around En=0.1-0.4 MeV, which was   9228 1451   81
smoothed in the ENDF/B-VI evaluation, was restored in the Version 9228 1451   82
VII evaluation. Also, the evaluation was adjusted slightly        9228 1451   83
between 1.0 and 2.5 MeV to better represent the covariance        9228 1451   84
analysis. Above 2.8 MeV, nubar in ENDF/B-VII differs from ENDF/B- 9228 1451   85
VI by a factor of 1.0004.                                         9228 1451   86
   Only limited experimental data has been obtained since the     9228 1451   87
ENDF/B-VI analysis.  The new measurements include data by         9228 1451   88
Khokhlov et al. (Kh94) and Boykov et al. (Bo90), but these        9228 1451   89
measurements are consistent with our earlier covariance analysis  9228 1451   90
(within experimental uncertainties) and are not expected to       9228 1451   91
modify our results significantly.                                 9228 1451   92
                                                                  9228 1451   93
MT=458:  ENERGY RELEASE FROM FISSION                              9228 1451   94
   Modifications were made to MT=458 based on a new analysis by   9228 1451   95
Madland (Ma06). The average total fission product kinetic energy  9228 1451   96
(EFR) and the average total prompt fission gamma-ray energy (EGP) 9228 1451   97
were taken from the Madland analysis. The average total prompt    9228 1451   98
fission neutron kinetic energy (ENP) was obtained from NJOY,      9228 1451   99
using the MF=5,MT=18 fission neutron spectra and prompt nubar     9228 1451  100
(MT=1,MT=456) from the evaluation. (This value of total neutron   9228 1451  101
energy is close to Madland's result.) The kinetic energy of       9228 1451  102
delayed fission neutrons (END), the total energy from delayed     9228 1451  103
gamma rays (EGD), the total energy released by delayed betas      9228 1451  104
(EB), and the energy released by neutrinos (ENU) were carried     9228 1451  105
over from the ENDF/B-VI evaluation. The total energy release per  9228 1451  106
fission is: ET = EFR+ENP+END+EGP+EGD+EB+ENU, and the quantity ER  9228 1451  107
(total energy less the neutrino energy, or pseudo-Q value) is     9228 1451  108
simply ET-ENU. ER is also included as the fission Q-value in      9228 1451  109
MF=3, MT=18,19,20,21,38.                                          9228 1451  110
   The uncertainties in EFR, ENP, END, EGP, EGD, EB, ENU, ER, and 9228 1451  111
ET are carried over from ENDF/B-VI.                               9228 1451  112
   There is currently no format for including energy dependence   9228 1451  113
in the ENDF/B file.  These are included in the NJOY processing.   9228 1451  114
                                                                  9228 1451  115
MT=460: BETA-DELAYED FISSION GAMMA DATA                           9228 1451  116
   See D.A. Brown (Br06). Evaluated by J. Pruet, based on Pruet   9228 1451  117
et al. (Pr04).                                                    9228 1451  118
