              ----- GENERAL INFORMATION -----                     9228 1451  316
   The maximum energy of the evaluation remains at 20 MeV, the    9228 1451  317
same as ENDF/B-VI.                                                9228 1451  318
   The total (MT=1), fission (MT=18), and radiative capture       9228 1451  319
(MT=102) cross sections are based mainly on experimental data,    9228 1451  320
complimented by nuclear model calculations. Model parameters for  9228 1451  321
the calculations were obtained by optimization with experimental  9228 1451  322
data. The neutron total and (n,f) cross section revisions include 9228 1451  323
new experimental data that were not included in the ENDF/B-VI     9228 1451  324
235U analysis.                                                    9228 1451  325
   The present evaluation utilizes some data from the ENDF/B-VI   9228 1451  326
evaluation, in particular, discrete inelastic scattering data for 9228 1451  327
levels below an excitation energy of 1.1 MeV. That is, the MT=51- 9228 1451  328
71 data in ENDF/B-VII correspond to the MT=51-82 data in ENDF/B-  9228 1451  329
VI, or combinations thereof.  The discrete (n,n') cross sections  9228 1451  330
and angular distributions in ENDF/B-VI are based on nuclear       9228 1451  331
theory/model code calculations with the ECIS70 coupled-channels   9228 1451  332
optical model code (Ra70) and with the GNASH (Ar88, Yo77) and     9228 1451  333
COMNUC (Du71) Hauser-Feshbach codes, with model parameters        9228 1451  334
optimized to experimental data. The GNASH calculations also       9228 1451  335
include preequilibrium contributions. The coupled-channels        9228 1451  336
optical model potential used is potential #3 in the International 9228 1451  337
Atomic Energy Agency's Reference Input Parameter Library (RIPL-2) 9228 1451  338
optical model parameter library (see Yo94).                       9228 1451  339
   DWBA calculations were performed with the DWUCK code (Ku70)    9228 1451  340
for several vibrational levels, using B(El) values inferred from  9228 1451  341
(d,d') data on U234, U235, U238, as well as Coulomb excitation    9228 1451  342
measurements.  A weak coupling model (Pe69) was used to apply the 9228 1451  343
U234 and U238 results to states in U235. A preliminary            9228 1451  344
description of the ENDF/B-VI analysis was given at the Mito       9228 1451  345
conference (Yo88).                                                9228 1451  346
   The neutron total cross section (MT=1) below 25 keV is also    9228 1451  347
the same as ENDF/B-VI.                                            9228 1451  348
   An updated 235U analysis was performed with the ECIS94 (Ra94)  9228 1451  349
and GNASH codes (Yo98) for ENDF/B-VII. This new analysis provide  9228 1451  350
the basis for the ENDF/B-VII evaluation of the following data:    9228 1451  351
MF=3,6 MT=16,17,37,91. Additionally, direct reaction cross        9228 1451  352
sections and angular distributions, inferred from neutron         9228 1451  353
spectrum measurements on 238U, are included for groups of states  9228 1451  354
in the MT=72-90 data.                                             9228 1451  355
                                                                  9228 1451  356
           ----- DETAILS OF LANL REVISION -----                   9228 1451  357
                                                                  9228 1451  358
MT=1: TOTAL CROSS SECTION                                         9228 1451  359
   2.25 - 25 keV:  Obtained from the ENDF/B-VI evaluation.        9228 1451  360
Corresponds to the unresolved resonance region.                   9228 1451  361
   25 keV - 20 MeV:  The previous ENDF/B-VI evaluation of the     9228 1451  362
neutron total cross                                               9228 1451  363
section in the MeV region resulted from a covariance analysis     9228 1451  364
with the GLUCS code (He80) of the experimental data available at  9228 1451  365
that time. Experimental data used include Fo71, Ve80, Bo71, Po81, 9228 1451  366
Gr73, Sc74, Po83, Pe60, Wh65, Ca73, Ga60, and Br58. The present   9228 1451  367
revision is an extension of the previous results by incorporation 9228 1451  368
of the experimental data of Lisowski (Li90) into the GLUCS        9228 1451  369
analysis.  The result of this analysis is a general lowering of   9228 1451  370
the total cross section by a few tenths of a percent above 50     9228 1451  371
keV.  The new result is lower than the previous one by 0.4% at 3  9228 1451  372
MeV, is unchanged at 8 MeV, and is lowered by 0.5% at 14 MeV and  9228 1451  373
by 1.3% (maximum change) at 20 MeV.                               9228 1451  374
                                                                  9228 1451  375
MT=2: ELASTIC SCATTERING CROSS SECTION                            9228 1451  376
  The elastic cross section is obtained by subtracting the        9228 1451  377
nonelastic cross section (MT=3) from the total cross section      9228 1451  378
(MT=1).                                                           9228 1451  379
                                                                  9228 1451  380
MT=3: NONELASTIC CROSS SECTION                                    9228 1451  381
   The (redundant) nonelastic cross section is the sum of the     9228 1451  382
following reactions: MT=4,16,17,18,37,102.                        9228 1451  383
                                                                  9228 1451  384
MT=4: INELASTIC CROSS SECTION                                     9228 1451  385
   Sum of MT=51-91.                                               9228 1451  386
                                                                  9228 1451  387
MT=16: (n,2n) CROSS SECTION                                       9228 1451  388
   The revised (n,xn) cross sections (and energy-angle            9228 1451  389
distributions) result from a revision of the GNASH nuclear model  9228 1451  390
code (Yo98) analysis that corrects an error in the analysis used  9228 1451  391
for the previous ENDF/B-VI evaluation.  The major change in the   9228 1451  392
analysis is a correction of an inconsistent treatment of          9228 1451  393
preequilibrium effects in the presence of fission that was        9228 1451  394
present in previous versions of the GNASH code.  The changes to   9228 1451  395
the cross sections are not large but are non-negligible.  For     9228 1451  396
example, changes in the (n,2n) cross section are +6% near 8 MeV,- 9228 1451  397
5% at 12 MeV, +3.5% at 14 MeV, and +16% at 20 MeV.  The new GNASH 9228 1451  398
analysis also results in modified energy-angle distributions in   9228 1451  399
MF = 6.                                                           9228 1451  400
   The revised (n,2n) cross section is in good agreement with the 9228 1451  401
experimental data of Becker et al. (Be98), Frehaut et al. (Fr80), 9228 1451  402
and Mathur et al. (Ma69,Ma72).  The Becker data are based on new  9228 1451  403
experimental data from a LANSCE-GEANIE experiment. The new        9228 1451  404
results are deduced from a combination of measured partial gamma- 9228 1451  405
ray cross sections and enhanced Hauser-Feshbach reaction          9228 1451  406
modeling.                                                         9228 1451  407
                                                                  9228 1451  408
MT=17,37: (n,xn) CROSS SECTIONS                                   9228 1451  409
   The (n,xn) cross sections are based on an analysis with the    9228 1451  410
GNASH nuclear model code (Yo98) that corrects an error in the     9228 1451  411
calculations used for the previous ENDF/B-VI evaluation.  See     9228 1451  412
comments under MT=16 above.                                       9228 1451  413
                                                                  9228 1451  414
MT=18: FISSION NEUTRON CROSS SECTION                              9228 1451  415
   2.25-25 keV:  Incorporated the cross section from Leal's       9228 1451  416
analysis in the unresolved resonance region, after                9228 1451  417
renormalization of the fission cross section (-2%) to agree with  9228 1451  418
the average of the ENDF/B-VII 235U(n,f) standard cross section in 9228 1451  419
this energy range.                                                9228 1451  420
   25 keV - 20 MeV:  The 235U(n,f) Version VII standard cross     9228 1451  421
section (Pr05) was incorporated into the 235U evaluation with     9228 1451  422
minimal smoothing.  The original standard energy grid is included 9228 1451  423
as a subset of a larger grid.  The expansion to the denser grid   9228 1451  424
was accomplished using a spline fit to a log-log file of the      9228 1451  425
standard data.                                                    9228 1451  426
   The Q-value was changed from 193.72 MeV to 193.4834 MeV to     9228 1451  427
maintain consistency with the MF=1,MT=458 data.                   9228 1451  428
                                                                  9228 1451  429
MT=19,20,21,38: MULTI-CHANCE FISSION CROSS SECTIONS               9228 1451  430
  The ratios of the multi-chance fission cross sections to the    9228 1451  431
total fission cross section were obtained from GNASH              9228 1451  432
calculations.  The evaluated multi-chance fission cross sections  9228 1451  433
were then obtained by multiplying the MT=18 total fission cross   9228 1451  434
section by the ratios.                                            9228 1451  435
   The Q-value was changed from 193.72 MeV to 193.4834 MeV to     9228 1451  436
maintain consistency with the MF=1,MT=458 data.                   9228 1451  437
                                                                  9228 1451  438
MT=51-71 DISCRETE INELASTIC LEVEL CROSS SECTIONS                  9228 1451  439
   The cross sections for MT=51-71 are obtained from the ENDF/B-  9228 1451  440
VI levels data or combinations thereof. The correspondance        9228 1451  441
between the level data in ENDF/B-VII and ENDF/B-VI is as follows: 9228 1451  442
        ENDF/B-VII       ENDF/B-VI                                9228 1451  443
         MT=51-56        MT=51-56                                 9228 1451  444
         MT=57           MT57 + MT58                              9228 1451  445
         MT=58           MT=59                                    9228 1451  446
         MT=59           MT60 + MT61 +MT62                        9228 1451  447
         MT=60           MT=63                                    9228 1451  448
         MT=61           MT64 + MT65                              9228 1451  449
         MT=62           MT66 + MT67                              9228 1451  450
         MT=63           MT68 + MT69                              9228 1451  451
         MT=64           MT70 + MT71 + MT72                       9228 1451  452
         MT=65           MT73 + MT74                              9228 1451  453
         MT=66           MT75 + MT76                              9228 1451  454
         MT=67           MT=77                                    9228 1451  455
         MT=68           MT=78                                    9228 1451  456
         MT=69           MT=79                                    9228 1451  457
         MT=70           MT=80                                    9228 1451  458
         MT=71           MT=81 + MT82                             9228 1451  459
   The levels MT=53,56,58,60 are the 9/2-, 11/2-, 13/2-, 15/2-    9228 1451  460
members of the 7/2- ground-state rotational band.  The cross      9228 1451  461
sections for these levels contain direct contributions calculated 9228 1451  462
with the ECIS70 code (Ra70).  The levels defined by MT=51-66 all  9228 1451  463
contain compound nucleus contributions calculated with the COMNUC 9228 1451  464
code (Du71).  The level cross sections corresponding to MT=67-71  9228 1451  465
are based on distorted wave Born approximation calculations with  9228 1451  466
the DWUCK code (Ku70) for 2+ and 3- vibrational states.           9228 1451  467
                                                                  9228 1451  468
MT=72-90: DISCRETE INELASTIC LEVEL CROSS SECTIONS (DIRECT         9228 1451  469
          REACTIONS TO GROUPS OF STATES)                          9228 1451  470
   The cross sections for MT=72-90 are based upon Baba's neutron  9228 1451  471
emission spectra measurements (Ba89) at 14 MeV for n + 238U       9228 1451  472
reactions.  To accomplish this, we adopted in the present         9228 1451  473
evaluation the MT=72-90 data from the ENDF/B-VII 238U evaluation  9228 1451  474
(MAT=9237).  To free up the necessary MT slots in the present     9228 1451  475
evaluation, it was necessary to combine data for some of the      9228 1451  476
weaker levels, as tabulated above.                                9228 1451  477
   The 238U results are based on DWBA calculations run with the   9228 1451  478
ECIS94 code (Ra94), assuming a set of 2+ or 3- (mainly)           9228 1451  479
vibrational states.  Deformation parameters were determined by    9228 1451  480
matching the 14-MeV Baba data. The tabulated cross sections are   9228 1451  481
actually sums of contributions to several states.  The DWBA       9228 1451  482
calculations were used to extrapolate the 14-MeV cross sections   9228 1451  483
to lower and higher energies, and to obtain the MF=4 angular      9228 1451  484
distributions for each assumed state. The spins, parities, and    9228 1451  485
deformation parameters used in the calculations are given in the  9228 1451  486
table below. These results affect the evaluation in the           9228 1451  487
excitation energy range Ex=1.17-3.91 MeV.                         9228 1451  488
